Abstract
In this work, the biological polysaccharide-based antioxidant polyglucose-sorbitol-carboxymethyl ether (PSC) was used as the precursor to synthesize $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles, which are expected to scavenge excess reactive oxygen species (ROS) to inhibit osteogenesis and promote osteoclast differentiation in iron accumulation (IA)-related osteoporosis. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles obtained were of a uniform particle size of $7.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ with elemental $\mathrm{{O/Fe/Cl/C}}$ at a ratio of 190:7:2:88. In addition, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles showed the ability to supply equivalent amounts of iron as the typical iron agent ferric ammonium citrate (FAC) in vitro and in vivo. Importantly, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@$ PSC nanoparticles not only induced antioxidative MC3T3-E1 and Raw 264.7 cells to scavenge ROS but also promoted osteogenic differentiation by activating Akt-GSK- $3\beta-\overrightarrow{1}$ -catenin and inhibiting osteoclast differentiation by inhibiting the $\mathbf{N}_{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{\mathrm{r}}}\mathbf{K}$ and NF- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot\kappa\mathrm{B}}$ pathways in vitro. In vivo, no IA-related osteoporosis was induced in a mouse model when enough iron was supplied by the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles. Overall, the biological polysaccharide-based antioxidant PSC can supply iron and prevent IA-related osteoporosis, indicating that it is a promising novel iron agent for applications to treat iron deficiency diseases.
Keywords: polyglucose-sorbitol-carboxymethyl ether; antioxidant; iron accumulation
## 1. Introduction
Iron accumulation (IA) is a state in which excess iron (Fe) in vivo exceeds the transferrin binding capacity; free iron not bound by transferrin appears in the blood, sinking into the tissue and accumulating in masses, resulting in tissue lesions[1-3]. Clinical evidence suggests that IA occurs during iron supplementation used to treat iron deficiency anemia[4], certain hereditary anemic diseases[5-7] and
postmenopausal women[8, 9]. As one of the most common factors of IA, iron supplementation is a common therapeutic method that has been widely used in iron deficiency anemia, which ranks among the world health organization's top 10 targeted global health problems, accounting for 2.4 percent of the global burden of disease[10, 11]. In population-based clinical trials, participants who received either oral or intravenous iron therapy had a significantly increased risk of osteoporosis, which indicates that IA is a dependent risk factor for osteoporosis[4]. Under normal circumstances, bone formation by osteoblasts and resorption by osteoclasts are maintained in a dynamic balance state to maintain bone homeostasis[12]. During the pathological development of IA, excess free iron ions sink into bone tissues and subsequently stimulate cells to generate excessive reactive oxygen species (ROS) to negatively regulate the bone metabolism balance, represented by the inhibition of osteogenesis and promotion of osteoclast differentiation[13-15]. When the balance in bone metabolism is disrupted, bone mass becomes damaged, and osteoporosis occurs. In summary, reducing the production of non-transferrin-binding iron ions and inhibiting the generation of excess ROS during the iron the supplementation process are key ways to prevent the occurrence of IA-related osteoporosis.
Conventional ion-type supplements, which could directly deliver iron ions to extracellular matrix such as ferrous sulfate, contain iron in the form of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , which is easily absorbed in vivo, but the increase in $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ can lead to an iron accumulation environment[16]. Therefore, a new generation of iron agents is based on trivalent iron, represented by ferric ammonium citrate (FAC)[17, 18]. FAC is used based on the
strategy that $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is difficult to absorb; nevertheless, it can only partly solve the problem of IA-related osteoporosis because cannot positively regulating bone metabolism or scavenge existing ROS[19]. Additionally, the poor bioutilization of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ is due to the difficulty it has crossing membranes and entering the cytoplasm[20, 21]. As reported previously, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles can be directly taken up into cellular lysosomes and release $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ for cells to use[22-24]. Additionally, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles have a longer half-life than ion-type supplements and can $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{-1}\mathbf{1}}\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{-3}}$ the cell membrane in different ways, which indicates the diversity of iron supplementation modalities [24, 25]. Thus, we chose $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles as the core because they can steadily release iron to treat iron deficiency-related diseases. Recently, natural macromolecule polymers, especially polysaccharides, have raised much concern due to their ability to scavenge ROS while satisfying biocompatibility[26, 27].
Polyglucose is a macromolecule that can be polymerized from dextran and has been widely applied in clinical treatment. It has been reported that the $\mathfrak{a}$ -glucosome at the end of dextran can be reduced to sorbitol and that some -OH can be carboxymethylated, which can further be used to prepare the macromolecules of polyglucose-sorbitol-carboxymethyl ether (PSC). In a previous report, PSC significantly scavenged ROS in vitro[28]. However, there is no work that shows whether PSC can be used as the precursor to prepare polysaccharide-based $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticles to scavenge excess ROS and thus inhibit osteogenesis and promote osteoclast differentiation in IA-related osteoporosis. Thus, we expect that the prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles will prevent IA-related osteoporosis by positively
regulating bone metabolism by producing fewer non-transferrin-binding iron ions scavenging ROS, while directly supplying intracellular elemental Fe.
In our work, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles (NPs) were prepared using polyglucose-sorbitol-carboxymethyl ether as a precursor according to a previously reported method[29] and characterized by various technologies. Then, the ability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{PSC}$ nanoparticles to scavenge ROS and their activation of the cellular antioxidant Nrf2/HO-1 pathway was investigated in vitro. The positive regulatory effect of these NPs on osteogenic and osteoclast differentiation was systemically evaluated, and the possible mechanisms were investigated. Finally, we applied the NPs in vivo using experimental mice to investigate their effect on IA-related osteoporosis and compared it to that of the typical iron agent ferric ammonium citrate (FAC).
## 2. Materials and Methods
## 2.1 Materials
Polyglucose-sorbitol-carboxymethyl ether (PSC), dextran, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich Life Sciences and High Technology Group, Inc. (MO, USA); FAC was supplied by Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China).
## 2.2 Preparation of the $F e_{2}O_{3}@P S C$ nanoparticles (NPs)
PSC modified from dextran was used to coat $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ following an established protocol[29]. NPs were synthesized by hydrocooling and magnetic internal heating coprecipitation using ferrous chloride hexahydrate and ferrous chloride tetrahydrate (Sigma-Aldrich, MO, USA) as precursors. Briefly, $0.2{\mathrm{~g~}}$ PSC was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water, and then a mixture of $0.06\mathrm{g}\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ and $0.03\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water was added. After this mixture was cooled to $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{~1~g~}$ of $28\%$ (w/v) ammonium hydroxide was added and stirred for more than 2 min. This mixture was heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 hour and then dialyzed for 24 hours with five cycles of ultrafiltration using a $100\mathrm{{kDa}}$ membrane.
## 2.3 Characterization of the NPs
The prepared NPs were primarily characterized by TEM (JEM-200CX, JEOL, Japan). TEM samples were prepared by placing a few drops of the aqueous dispersions on carbon-coated copper grids and drying them at room temperature. The hydrodynamic diameters and zeta potential of the NPs were measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS) with a particle size analyzer (Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS90, UK). The distribution of hydrodynamic diameter of the NPs in different solutions was measured using dynamic light scattering. An X-ray diffractometer (X'tra, ARL) was used to detect the NP structure. Energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) element mapping was performed to verify the connection of the iron core and PSC polymer.
## 2.4 Cell Culture
MC3T3-E1 and Raw264.7 cell lines were separately used to simulate osteoblast and osteoclast cells and were maintained in $\mathfrak{a}$ -MEM and high-glucose DMEM. Primary monocytes were isolated from 8-week-old C57BL/6 mice in a sterile environment and maintained in $\mathfrak{a}$ -MEM medium. All media were supplemented with $10\%$ (v/v) fetal bovine serum (FBS) and $100\mathrm{U/mL}$ penicillin and streptomycin
(Gibco, USA) and changed every 3 days. Cells were maintained in an incubator with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The cell density was maintained at $70\%80\%$ , and logarithmic growth cells were used for experiments. When cells were seeded in plates, a cell counter (Millipore, Germany) was used to control the uniformity between duplicate wells.
## 2.5 Evaluation of Cell Viability
Cell viability was analyzed using a Cell Counting-8 kit (KeyGen Biotech, China). MC3T3-E1 and RAW264.7 cells were seeded into 96-well plates at a density of $3{\times}10^{3}$ cells per well. After adherence, the cells were separately treated with FAC and the NPs at different concentrations (12, 25, 50, 100, 200, 500, and $1000\upmu\mathrm{M},$ ). After incubation for 24 hours, $10\%$ v/v CCK-8 solution was added to the medium. After another 2 hours, the optical density (OD) of each group was measured at $450\mathrm{nm}$ using a microplate reader (Thermo, USA).
## 2.6 Evaluation of intracellular Fe
Intracellular Fe quantitation was performed by Perl’s blue staining and inductively coupled plasma (ICP) methods. The cells were fixed with $4\%$ (v/v) formaldehyde (PFA), and a Perl’s blue staining assay was performed to determine the internalization of FAC and the NPs. Nuclear fast red solution was used to stain the nucleus, and then, the cells were observed and imaged by microscopy (Axiovert 40, Zeiss, Germany). The cells were collected under the same experimental conditions. Then, the concentration of iron in the cells was determined by inductively coupled plasma emission spectrometry.
## 2.7 Evaluation of ROS
The effects of FAC and the NPs were evaluated by flow cytometry and fluorescence imaging. MC3T3-E1 and Raw 264.7 cells were divided into 6 groups: Blank (phosphate-buffered saline (PBS)), FAC ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ FAC), NPs ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ NPs), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}{+}\mathrm{PB}\mathrm{S}$ ${\mathrm{(}}100{\upmu}{\mathrm{M}}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}{\mathrm{,}}$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{FAC}$ (100 μM H2O2+100 μM FAC) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{+NPs}$ $100\upmu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{+}100\upmu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{N}$ Ps) groups. In brief, after stimulation with FAC or the NPs for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the cells were washed with PBS and then incubated in the dark for 5 minutes in Krebs-Ringer solution containing 5 μM DCFH-DA. After washing three times with PBS, the cells were immediately evaluated by flow cytometry (BD Accuri C6, USA) and photographed by a fluorescence microscope (ZEN, Germany). The peak excitation wavelength for oxidized DCF was $492{\mathrm{nm}}$ , and the emission wavelength was 527 nm. Each determination was based on the mean fluorescence intensity of 10,000 cells.
## 2.8 Induction and evaluation of osteogenic differentiation
Osteogenic differentiation was performed with MC3T3-E1 cell lines. In addition to the medium used to culture cells, $10\mathrm{mM}\upbeta$ -sodium glycerophosphate and 50 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ vitamin C were added to stimulate osteogenic differentiation. The cells were divided into 4 groups: blank (without osteogenic induction), control (PBS treated), FAC ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ FAC) and NP ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ NPs) groups. Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) levels and alizarin red S (ARS) staining were separately tested at 7 and 28 days to evaluate the generation of osteoblasts and the formation of calcium nodules. Quantitative PCR was used to test the expression of OCN, ALP, Col1α1 and Runx2.
$\upbeta$ -Catenin, Akt (total and phosphorylated), Jnk (total and phosphorylated), GSK-3β (total and phosphorylated), Col1α1, Runx2 and p65 were tested by western blot analysis.
## 2.9 Induction and evaluation of osteoclast differentiation
Osteoclast differentiation was performed by primary monocytes isolated from 8-week-old C57BL/6 mice. Briefly, the tibia and femur were carefully removed from the mice and placed in sterile PBS solution. Then, the bone morrow was washed with medium containing $30\mathrm{ng/mL}$ M-CSF. When the cell density reached $90\mathrm{-}100\%$ , 50 ng/mL RANKL and Fe-containing solutions were added to the medium. Three groups, the blank (PBS only), FAC ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ FAC) and NP ( $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ NPs) groups, were established. TRAP staining, quantitative $^\mathrm{\Delta}$ and western blotting were performed until the osteoclasts could be observed by microscopy. Quantitative PCR indexes included CTSK, NFATc1, OSCAR, TRAP5 and CALR, while protein indexes included NFATc1, c-Fos, Erk (total and phosphorylation), Jnk (total and phosphorylated), p38 (p38 mitogen-activated protein kinase, total and phosphorylated) and p65 (nuclear factor kappa-B p65).
## 2.10 Animal Management
Forty-two 8-week-old ICR female mice purchased from the Nanjing Medical University Medical Animal Experimental Base were randomly allocated into 6 groups $\mathrm{{(n{=}7}}/$ group). Three groups of mice were bilaterally ovariectomized and received intraperitoneal injections of normal saline $\mathrm{\DeltaNS+OVX}$ , $100\mathrm{mg/kg}$ ferric ammonium citrate $(\mathrm{FAC+OVX})$ ) or 100 mg/kg NPs $(\mathrm{NPs+OVX})$ twice per week for 2 months.
Another three groups of mice only received intraperitoneal injections of the same three liquids (NS, FAC, and NPs). All mice were sacrificed 2 days after the final injection. Serum, femur, tibia and viscus (heart, liver, spleen, lung and kidney) samples were collected and maintained at $-80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for later use. All mice were kept in a specific pathogen-free environment with bedding and food changed twice per week. All animal experiments were approved by the Drum Tower Hospital Ethics Committee (2019AE01038). All animal experiments complied with the ARRIVE guidelines and were carried out in accordance with the U.K. Animals (Scientific Procedures) Act of 1986 and associated guidelines, EU Directive 2010/63/EU for animal experiments, and the National Institutes of Health Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals (NIH Publications No. 8023, revised 1978).
2.11 Establishment of the postmenopausal osteoporosis mouse model
After gas anesthesia, the mice were fixed, and skin was preserved. Bilateral dorsal incisions were performed, and the ovaries were located. After the fallopian tubes were ligated by silk suture (7-0) to prevent possible bleeding, the ovaries were removed. In the sham group, a piece of par-ovarian adipose tissue was removed instead of the ovaries. Finally, the wound was sutured in a layered manner, and antibiotics were used to prevent infection.
## 2.12 Microcomputed tomography (micro-CT) analysis and radiography
The distal region of the right femur was preliminarily observed by an X-ray digital imaging system (Faxitron, MX-20, USA). Then, the distal femur and proximal tibia were subjected to micro-CT analysis. Bones were analyzed on a Skyscan 1172 high-resolution micro-CT scanner (Scanco Medical, Switzerland) using a 9- $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ voxel size, $59\mathrm{KVp}$ , $127\upmu\mathrm{A}$ , and $0.4^{\circ}$ rotation step. Regions of interest (ROIs) were defined as $360~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ from the end of the distal growth plate away from the diaphysis. Cortical and trabecular indexes were separately calculated. 3D morphological parameters were determined with the original system of micro-CT following the manufacturer’s protocols, included BMC, BMD, bone volume/total volume (BV/TV), trabecular number (Tb. N), trabecular separation (Tb. Sp), trabecular thickness (Tb. Th), trabecular bone pattern factor (Tb. Pf), and the structure model index (SMI).
## 2.13 Evaluation of Serological Indicators
The serum iron and ALP levels were measured by an automatic biochemical analyzer (Rayto, Chemray 240, China). Serum ferritin (FE) and transferrin (TRF) were measured using a mouse ELISA kit (Cusabio, Wuhan, China) according to the manufacturer’s protocol. Bone turnover markers, including ALP, OSCAR, and PINP, were analyzed. ELISA kits for OSCAR and PINP were purchased from Cloud-Clone Corp, while the ALP kit has been previously described. Absorbance at $450\mathrm{nm}$ was measured for the contents of each well. The plate reader was calibrated according to the manufacturer’s specifications.
## 2.14 Histological sections and 2D-morphological parameter analysis
Three femur samples from each group were fixed in $4\%$ paraformaldehyde for 24 hours and then decalcified by a $10\%$ EDTA $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ solution until tissues could be easily impaled by a 22G needle. After dehydration and transparency, tissues were embedded in paraffin, and $3\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ sections were prepared. Next, H&E, TRAP and
Masson’s staining were performed. 2D morphological parameters including $\%$ Tb. Ar, OC. Ar and O. Pm were measured and calculated to reflect the situation of bone mass, bone erosion and bone formation, respectively.
## 2.15 Total RNA extraction, reverse transcription and quantitative real-time PCR
An RNA-Quick purification kit (Yishan Biotech, Cat. No. RN001) was used to extract total RNA from cells according to the manufacturer’s protocol. The total amount of RNA was authenticated and quantified by a spectrophotometer (OD-1000, OneDrop, China). Specifically, $1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ of RNA sample was reverse-transcribed using a Prime Script RT reagent kit (TaKaRa, Cat. No. RR037A) according to the established protocol. Twenty microliters of a qPCR reaction system contained $1\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of diluted cDNA, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of sybr green qPCR master $\mathrm{m}_{i_{\star}}$ (Vazyme Biotech, China), $0.6~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of each forward and reverse primer $\mathrm{\dot{a}.\dot{\ v d}}7.8~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of RNase-free water. The forward and reverse primers are listed in Table S1. Amplifications were carried out in a LightCycler 480 II (Roche, Switzerland) under the following conditions: denaturation for $3\mathrm{min}$ at $94^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by cycles of 20 s denaturation at $94^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 20 s annealing at the primer specific temperature and 15 s elongation at $72^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ Quantitative analysis was performed by the manufacturer’s software following the given protocols.
## 2.16 Preparation of the protein samples and western blot analysis
Cell and tissue samples were lysed by RIPA lysis buffer with phosphatase inhibitors, protease inhibitor and PMSF. Nucleoprotein and cytoplasmic protein were separated and extracted by a commercial kit. Then, the lysates were centrifuged at $12{,}000\ \mathrm{g}$ for $20\mathrm{min}$ , and the supernatant was used. A PierceTM BCA protein assay kit was used to quantify the concentration of protein following the manufacturer’s instructions. The same amount of protein was subjected to western blot analysis after mixing with loading buffer at $95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Proteins were separated by size with $10\%$ gel electrophoresis and transferred to Immobilon-P transfer membrane. The membrane was blocked with $5\%$ DifcoTM skim milk/TBS-T for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ and then soaked in the respective primary antibodies listed in Table. S2 at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. After washing in TBS-T for $3{\times}10\mathrm{min}$ , the membrane was incubated with a horseradish peroxidase-conjugated secondary antibody for 1 hour at room temperature. Next, the membrane was subjected to $5{\times}5$ min washes in TBS-T, and chemiluminescence was detected by PierceTM ECL western blotting substrate in a Tanon 5200-Multi Chemiluminescent Imaging System (TanonTM, China).
## 2.17 Statistical analysis
Data are reported as the means ± standard deviation (SD). To calculate the alter in percentage way, we first calculated the mean of control group and marked it as $X_{1}$ . Values in experimental $\tilde{\mathfrak{c}}^{\mathsf{r}_{\mathbf{0}\mathbf{u}}}\mathsf{p}$ was marked as $\mathbf{A}_{1}$ , A2… We separately calculated the value of $\mathbf{A}_{1}-\mathbf{X}_{1},\mathbf{A}_{2}-\mathbf{\Delta}_{1}$ as $\mathbf{B}_{1}$ , ${\bf B}_{2}$ … Finally, we use statistical analysis to calculate the mean $\pm$ SD of ${\bf B}_{1}$ , B2…
The statistical significance of differences between each group was determined using a t-test (2 groups) and one-way ANOVA followed by Tukey’s post hoc analysis (more than 2 groups). A significant difference was defined as $\mathtt{p}<0.05$ . Statistical analysis was performed by SPSS 20.0, and a statistical graph was drawn by GraphPad 8.0 software.
## 3. Results
3.1 Preparation and Characterization of the $F e_{2}O_{3}@P S C$ nanoparticles $(N P s)$ .
In the current study, our as-prepared NPs consisted of an $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoparticle core and a PSC shell (Fig. 1a). PSC was produced from dextran. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image in Fig. 1b shows that the as-prepared IONPs (iron oxide nanoparticles) dispersed quite uniformly, and we observed that the mean particle size was approximately $7.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ (mean diameter, $7.3\pm0.4\mathrm{nm}(\dot{\bar{\mathbf{\mu}}}.\dot{\mathbf{u}}.),\mathbf{\mu}_{\mathrm{{L}}}=321$ particles) in Fig. 1c. Additionally, the hydrodynamic diameters of the particles were measured by dynamic light scattering (DLS), and the mean size was approximately $30.3\mathrm{nm}$ (mean diameter, $30.3\pm0.2\mathrm{nm}$ (s.d.)) in Fig. 1d. The X-ray diffraction pattern shows that ferric oxide crystals were indeed typical $\cdot\mathrm{e}_{2}\cdot\sum_{3}$ . The characteristic diffraction peaks of the XRD pattern were consistent with the standard $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ pattern (JCPDS 33-0664), and the sharp diffraction peaks reflected the crystal properties of the particles in Fig. 1e. The energy dispersive $\mathsf{s}_{\mathbf{r}}$ ectroscopy (EDS) element mapping images and EDS spectra of the NPs show $\mathbf{\hat{\Pi}}^{\mathbf{{\sigma}}_{\mathbf{{\hat{\Pi}}}},\mathbf{{\sigma}}_{\mathbf{{\hat{\Pi}}}}}$ that O, Fe, Cl and C elements were uniformly distributed in the microsphere. The element molar ratio of O, Fe, Cl and C is approximately 190:7:2:88 (Fig. 1f). The zeta potential of the particles was approximately $-20.9\mathrm{mV}$ (Fig. S1).
3.2 Fe2O3@PSC nanoparticles (NPs, 100 μmol/L) can supply similar amounts of iron as FAC in vitro.
First, the cell viability of the NPs and FAC in MC3T3-E1 and Raw264.7 cells was evaluated by CCK-8 assay. As shown in Fig. 2a, b, the viability was significant decreased for both kinds of cells in the FAC group when the concentration was higher than $100\mathrm{\:\upmumol/L}$ . With a treatment of $100\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ NPs, the viability increased to 115.5 and $139.7\%$ for the MC3T3-E1 and Raw264.7 cells, respectively. According to these data, $100\mathrm{\:\upmumol/L}$ NPs were used in all subsequent experiments without special modifications. The ICP results of the cell lysates are shown in Fig. 2c, d, the intracellular iron contents showed no difference between the NP and FAC groups at 1 and 24 hours, while the level of intracellular iron ions after treatment with the NPs for $24\mathrm{h}$ was obviously higher than it was at 1 hour, which indicated the iron-supplying effect of the NPs. The Prussian blue staining results are shown in Fig. 2. Fig. 2e, f also shows that the NPs had an iron-supplying effect similar to that of the typical iron agent FAC.
3.3 Fe2O3@PSC nanoparticles (NPs) can reverse the $F e^{3+}$ -induced increase in ROS by activating the phosphorylation of Nrf2 in vitro.
In the fluorescence microphotographs shown in Fig. 3a, stronger green fluorescence signals representing ROS were observed in the FAC-stimulated group with/without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ treatment, which is consistent with the flow cytometry results shown in Fig. 3b, c. With the treatment of the NPs, the level of the ROS returned to the normal level and decreased to 77.3 and $43.6\%$ in the MC3T3-E1 and Raw 264.7 cells, respectively, compared to the level in the corresponding FAC-treated groups. The expression of SOD1, INOS and NOX2 was related to oxidative stress (Fig. 3d, e) by flow cytometry showed consistent alteration. At the protein level (Fig. 3f, g, h), the phosphorylation of Nrf2 was activated, and the cellular antioxidant protein HO-1
increased in the NP-treated group. When the phosphorylation of Nrf2 was inhibited in the presence of the NPs, the cellular antioxidant protein HO-1 decreased significantly to the normal level.
3.4 Fe2O3@PSC nanoparticles (NPs) can reverse the $F e^{3+}$ -induced decrease in osteogenic differentiation in vitro
After incubation with NPs and FAC in osteogenic medium for 7 and 28 days, ALP level and ARS staining were measured in MC3T3-E1 cells. Fig. 4a shows the photographs of the staining. The inhibitory effect of FAC is observed, while NPs reversed the $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ -induced inhibition of osteogenic differentiation. The positive areas were then measured and calculated (Fig. 4d, e). Compared with the FAC group, the areas in the NP groups increased to 228.2 and $1538\%$ . The same trend was noted for the quantitation of ALP and ARS, as shown in Fig. 4f, g. As shown in Fig. 4b, the expression of bone markers (Runx2, Col1α1, OCN, and ALP) decreased in the FAC group but increased to levels higher than those in the control group at both the mRNA and protein levels in the NP group (Figs. 4i, k, and S2). The typical osteogenic differentiation-related Akt-GSK- $3\upbeta$ - $\upbeta$ -catenin pathway was inhibited by FAC, while it was activated by NPs, as indicated by the phosphorylation of Akt and GSK- $3\upbeta$ and the translocation of $\upbeta$ -catenin. Additionally, the NPs did not induce the phosphorylation of the apoptosis-related protein JNK in a manner similar to FAC (Fig. 4b, c, h, i, j).
3.5 Fe2O3@PSC nanoparticles (NPs) can decrease $F e^{3+}$ -induced formation of osteoclasts in vitro
After isolation from 8-week-old mice, monocytes were incubated with NPs, FAC and cytokines for 7 days. As is shown in Fig. 5a, b, c, the number and area of the newly formed osteoclasts showed a dose-dependent increase in the FAC groups but a decrease in the NP groups. When treated with $100\mathrm{\:\upmumol/L}$ NPs, the area and number of osteoclasts decreased to 19.9 and $36.6\%$ , respectively, compared to treatment with $100\mathrm{\:\upmumol/L}$ FAC. The osteoclast-related markers (CTSK, NFATc1, OSCAR, TRAP5 and CALR) showed an increase in the FAC-treated group but a decrease in the NP-treated group (Fig. 5h). To verify the possible mechanism of NPs on osteoclast differentiation, the phosphorylation of JNK, ERK and p38 in the MAPK pathway and the translocation of p65 in the NF- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\kappa\mathbf{B}}$ pathway were examined by western blotting. The results showed that both the MAPK and NF-κB pathways were activated by FAC but inhibited by the NPs, which led to the inhibition of osteoclast differentiation (Fig. 5d, e, f, g).
3.6 Fe2O3@PSC nanoparticles (NPs) can protect mice from IA-induced osteoporosis while supplying similar amounts of iron with FAC in vivo
Eight-week-old female mice were used to perform in vivo tests. Radiographic results, including X-ray and CT-reconstruction photographs of femurs and tibias, are separately displayed in Figs. 6a and S3a. The alteration in bone mass is directly reflected in the image. The bone mineral density of total volume (BMC) was calculated as shown in Fig. 6c. In the NP group, the BMC increased to 155.4 and $161.7\%$ in the Sham and OVX groups, respectively, compared to the FAC group. The changes in other bone trabecular parameters in the femurs and tibias are shown in Table 1 and Table S3. In the 2D-morphologic photographs, H&E, TRAP and
Masson’s staining photographs are observed in Fig. 6b. A semiquantitative analysis of the trabecular area, Oc. Ar and $\%0$ pm representing bone mass, osteoclast and osteogenic differentiation, respectively, were measured and calculated (Fig. 6d, e, f). The trend follows the changes in the BMC. Serum indexes including transferrin, ferritin and Fe levels showed that the NPs supplied iron similar to FAC (Fig. 6g, h, i). The increase in ALP and PINP in the NP group compared to the FAC group showed the increased ability of osteogenic differentiation, while the decrease in OSCAR showed the inhibition of osteoclast differentiation in vivo (Fig. 6j, k, l). During the experiment, the weight of the mice was not significantly different between groups (Fig. S4a). The function of the liver and kidney was reflected by serum indexes, including ALT, AST and BUN levels, and showed no differences (Fig. S4b, c, d). According to H&E staining results shown in Fig. S4e, no structural or pathological alterations were found in the heart, liver, spleen, lung or kidney between the different groups.
## 4. Discussion
In the clinic, iron supplementation is a common therapeutic method that has been widely used in iron deficiency-related diseases[10, 30]. Typical iron ion agents can achieve satisfactory therapeutic effects in these diseases, while severe side effects, such as IA-related osteoporosis, remain problems that need to be solved[4, 8, 19]. In our work, we tried to solve the problem of typical ion iron agents in two ways. First, we selected nanoscaled $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ to minimize the generation of free iron ions to prevent the occurrence of IA. As reported, an appropriate nanosized ferric agent can supply more iron ions at a lower speed, which may prevent the severe side effects caused by iron accumulation[31]. Additionally, we used an antioxidant polysaccharide, PSC, to protect bone tissues from the damaging effects caused by ROS generation induced by free iron ions. In a previous study, Jonathan J et al. used a nano-disperse ferritin-core mimetic to supply iron without causing oxidative damage to the digestive tract[24]. Considering this result, we referred to the classic chemical coprecipitation method used to produce ferumoxytol, which has been approved by the FDA as an inorganic nanodrug, to prepare novel $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@$ PSC nanoparticles[29, 32]. FAC was chosen as the representative traditional iron agent for comparative study, mainly because FAC is still in clinical use as a commonly used second-generation oral iron supplement[19] and is commonly used as an iron supplement in animal and cellular iron storage models[13, 33-35]. Compared to typical iron ion agents, the NPs sustainably released iron ions instead of releasing a great quantity in a short time. The characterization showed that PSC has been conjugated to the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ core.
After the preparation of NPs, we applied them in vitro and in vivo to confirm their biological activity. In the in vitro experiments, we first tested the biocompatibility of NPs and FAC. The results showed that with the stimulation of FAC, the cell viability showed a dose-dependent decrease, and the difference was significant when the concentration was $200\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}$ . Similar to a previous report, the NPs induced no cellular toxicity at concentrations ranging from 0 to $500\ \mathrm{\textmumol/L}[36]$ . Interestingly, with treatments of 50 and $100\mathrm{\:\upmumol/L}$ NPs, the cell viability increased significantly. The difference might be explained by a lower cell density in our work and the fact
that the NPs have the potential to stimulate cellular proliferation. In vivo, the functional indexes of the liver and kidney, and sections of the heart, liver, lung, spleen and kidney, showed that the NPs are biosafe and have the potential to be translated into the clinic.
Second, we evaluated the ROS-scavenging ability of the NPs. In our experiment, the NPs cleared ROS nonenzymatically and activated the Nrf2-related pathway. We found that with NP treatment, the ability of the cells to scavenge existing ROS significantly increased, as reflected by the decreased level in flow cytometry results. Phosphorylated Nrf2 can be translocated into the nucleus and bind to antioxidant reaction elements to produce antioxidases[37, 38]. HO-1, a representative antioxidase, can promote the translation of biliverdin to bilirubin and the generation of NO and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to scavenge ROS[39]. Additionally, another form of iron oxide, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , is promising as a way to clear ROS enzymatically [40, 41]. However, the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ released from $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ might cause more side effects than that released by $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , and whether iron ions release $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ in vivo still needs to be explored.
Considering the iron supply and ROS scavenging effect, we evaluated the effect of the NPs on bone metabolism. In our experiment, the NPs promoted osteogenic differentiation, while inhibiting osteoclast differentiation to positively regulate bone metabolism. The NPs could first promote the transfer from preosteoblasts to osteoblasts, as reflected by the increased ALP and Runx2 levels in 7 days. Mature osteoblasts can then mineralize and differentiate into osteocytes, which can be accessed by ARS staining at 28 days. Qiwei Wang et al. found that $100~\mathrm{\upmug/mL}$ IONPs promoted osteogenic differentiation of human bone-derived mesenchymal stem cells (hBMSCs) without osteogenic supplements in vitro, as indicated by the increase in ALP activity, ARS staining and osteogenic indexes, including RUNX2 and BMP2 [36]. Our results showed that with the treatment of osteogenic medium, the NPs did not further enhance osteogenic differentiation, which indicated that the NPs might enhance osteogenic differentiation by a mechanism similar to that of osteogenic supplements such as vitamin C and $\upbeta$ -sodium glycerophosphate. In our experiment, the osteogenic protection effect was related to the activation of the Akt-GSK- $3\upbeta$ - $\upbeta$ -catenin pathway. Briefly, the NPs induced the phosphorylation of Akt and GSK- $3\upbeta$ to enhance the translocation of $\scriptstyle\int-1$ atenin from the cytoplasm to the nucleus. Similar to some proinflammatory cytokines, iron ions have been shown to impair the Akt-GSK-3β- $\cdot\upbeta$ -catenin pathway and inhibit cellular proliferation, migration and differentiation[42]. According to the mRNA transcript expression levels shown in previous work, the phosphorylation of Akt increased significantly[36]. In osteoclast differentiation, with the treatment of NPs, both the area and the number of osteoclasts showed a dose-dependent decrease, indicating the inhibition of osteoclast differentiation. The inhibition of osteoclast-related MAPK and NF-κB pathways may explain this phenomenon[43]. In Li et al.’s study, a higher concentration $\mathrm{(0.1~mg}$ $\mathrm{Fe/mL}$ ) of IONPs was used to treat bone monocytes, and the osteoclast-inhibiting effect was apparent[43]. Our results were consistent with these findings, showing that the NPs obviously inhibited the formation of osteoclasts in a RANKL-induced environment. The different effects of the NPs on osteogenic and osteoclast
differentiation can be explained by the fact that osteoclast differentiation is more dependent on ROS in the inflammatory environment stimulated by RANKL. Additionally, in our study, we performed more comprehensive and detailed research on the effects of the NPs on bone metabolism, considering both osteogenic and osteoclast differentiation simultaneously. Combined with the effect of the cellular iron absorption of the NPs, we hypothesized that the effect of the NPs on osteogenesis and osteoclast differentiation might be additive, a combination of the positive effect of the NPs and the negative effect of iron ions released by the NPs, which ultimately showed a protective effect on bone metabolism (Fig. 7).
Considering the in vitro results, we applied the NPs in vivo. We used transferrin, ferritin and Fe to reflect the iron-supplying effect and found that the NPs achieved an effect similar to that of FAC. In the groups without OVX, bone mass was obviously reduced with FAC treatment, indicating the establishment of an IA-related osteoporosis model. Upon treatment of the NPs, although serum Fe did not seem to differ, osteoporosis did not occur. In addition, estrogen is another an important factor contributing to the incidence of osteoporosis, and it has been reported that postmenopausal women are more likely to have osteoporosis induced by iron accumulation[8, 44][45]. Thus, we prepared OVX models to test the bone protection effect of the NPs in an estrogen-deficient environment. With FAC treatment, osteoporosis tended to be more serious, while no difference between the NP and control groups was found, indicating that the NPs did not delay osteoporosis in the postmenopausal mice. The 2D morphological measurements showed that the
formation of osteoids increased, while the bone resorption area decreased, findings that also support the positively regulated effect of bone metabolism observed in vitro. In previous studies, osteogenic differentiation has not been proven in vivo. Examining osteoclast differentiation, Li Liu et al. intravenously injected $10\mathrm{mg/kg}$ IONPs in an OVX mouse model every two days and found that osteoclast differentiation was inhibited and bone mass density was enhanced [43]. However, in our experiment, the intraperitoneal injection of $100\mathrm{mg/kg}$ NPs had no significant therapeutic effect on osteoporosis. The difference might be explained by the low absorption efficiency of intraperitoneal vessels, which led to fewer NPs in bone tissue than was realized with the therapeutic dosage in our experiment.
## 5. Conclusion
In our experiment, we prepared the biological polysaccharide-based iron oxide nanoparticles (NPs) with $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot(\underline{{{\omega}}}_{\cdot}^{\prime}\mathrm{S}\underline{{{\mathrm{C}}}}$ . The NPs supplied amounts of iron similarly in vitro and vivo, while scavenging ROS by activating the cellular Nrf2-HO-1 pathway. In addition, the NPs positively regulated bone metabolism, promoted osteogenesis, and inhibited osteoclast differentiation, and protected the body from osteoporosis when used as an iron supplement in vitro and in vivo. Overall, these results indicate that the NPs may be considered promising iron agents for use to avoid IA-related osteoporosis.
